{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"06b291560ffb19e1","name":"card_unit_fiat_cr_32","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Italy/units/card_unit_fiat_cr_32","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"FIAT C.R.32","text":"When your HQ gains defense, fully repair a random friendly unit.","flavorText":"The FIAT C.R.32 was an Italian biplane fighter aircraft that saw significant use during the interwar period and the early years of WWII. It was designed by the Italian manufacturer FIAT and entered service with the Regia Aeronautica (Italian Royal Air Force) in 1933. During the Spanish Civil War (1936-1939), the FIAT C.R.32 gained its reputation as one of the best biplane fighters of its time. It demonstrated excellent maneuverability and agility, making it highly effective in aerial combat. Its robust construction and good diving capabilities also added to its combat ability. However, by the outbreak of WWII in 1939, the FIAT C.R.32 was already becoming outdated compared to the monoplane fighters that other nations were deploying. It was gradually phased out of frontline service in the Regia Aeronautica, being replaced by more modern aircraft like the Macchi C.200 and C.202. During the early stages of the war, the FIAT C.R.32 continued to see limited use by Italy and its allies and it was used in various combat theaters, including North Africa, the Mediterranean, and the Balkans. By the mid-1940s, the FIAT C.R.32 was mostly relegated to secondary roles, such as training, reconnaissance, and ground-attack missions. It was gradually phased out of service as more advanced aircraft became available. Nonetheless, it is worth noting that a few C.R.32s remained in use until the end of the war in 1945.","faction":"Italy","type":"fighter","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":3},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_fiat_cr_32","title":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_unit_fiat_cr_32_title","SourceString":"FIAT C.R.32","LocalizedString":"FIAT C.R.32"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":1,"defense":3,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Italy","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_unit_fiat_cr_32_text","SourceString":"When your HQ gains defense, fully repair a random friendly unit.","LocalizedString":"When your HQ gains defense, fully repair a random friendly unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_italy","Key":"card_unit_fiat_cr_32_history","SourceString":"The FIAT C.R.32 was an Italian biplane fighter aircraft that saw significant use during the interwar period and the early years of WWII. It was designed by the Italian manufacturer FIAT and entered service with the Regia Aeronautica (Italian Royal Air Force) in 1933. During the Spanish Civil War (1936-1939), the FIAT C.R.32 gained its reputation as one of the best biplane fighters of its time. It demonstrated excellent maneuverability and agility, making it highly effective in aerial combat. Its robust construction and good diving capabilities also added to its combat ability. However, by the outbreak of WWII in 1939, the FIAT C.R.32 was already becoming outdated compared to the monoplane fighters that other nations were deploying. It was gradually phased out of frontline service in the Regia Aeronautica, being replaced by more modern aircraft like the Macchi C.200 and C.202. During the early stages of the war, the FIAT C.R.32 continued to see limited use by Italy and its allies and it was used in various combat theaters, including North Africa, the Mediterranean, and the Balkans. By the mid-1940s, the FIAT C.R.32 was mostly relegated to secondary roles, such as training, reconnaissance, and ground-attack missions. It was gradually phased out of service as more advanced aircraft became available. Nonetheless, it is worth noting that a few C.R.32s remained in use until the end of the war in 1945.","LocalizedString":"The FIAT C.R.32 was an Italian biplane fighter aircraft that saw significant use during the interwar period and the early years of WWII. It was designed by the Italian manufacturer FIAT and entered service with the Regia Aeronautica (Italian Royal Air Force) in 1933. During the Spanish Civil War (1936-1939), the FIAT C.R.32 gained its reputation as one of the best biplane fighters of its time. It demonstrated excellent maneuverability and agility, making it highly effective in aerial combat. Its robust construction and good diving capabilities also added to its combat ability. However, by the outbreak of WWII in 1939, the FIAT C.R.32 was already becoming outdated compared to the monoplane fighters that other nations were deploying. It was gradually phased out of frontline service in the Regia Aeronautica, being replaced by more modern aircraft like the Macchi C.200 and C.202. During the early stages of the war, the FIAT C.R.32 continued to see limited use by Italy and its allies and it was used in various combat theaters, including North Africa, the Mediterranean, and the Balkans. By the mid-1940s, the FIAT C.R.32 was mostly relegated to secondary roles, such as training, reconnaissance, and ground-attack missions. It was gradually phased out of service as more advanced aircraft became available. Nonetheless, it is worth noting that a few C.R.32s remained in use until the end of the war in 1945."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BrothersInArms","isReserved":true,"EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnAfterOtherCardGainDefense"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundCue'AU_depl_biplane_Cue'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Biplane/AU_depl_biplane_Cue.0"},"soundMoveToFrontline":{"ObjectName":"SoundCue'AU_move_biplane_01_Cue'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Biplane/AU_move_biplane_01_Cue.0"},"soundAttack":{"ObjectName":"SoundCue'AU_move_biplane_01_Cue'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Biplane/AU_move_biplane_01_Cue.0"},"soundBlowUp":{"ObjectName"